    Taylor / Cauchy local reconstruction
        ↓
    compatible germs
        ↓
    path continuation
        ↓
    monodromy
        ↓
    Riemann-surface lift
        ↓
    maximal analytic continuation.

If a

    natural boundary

blocks further continuation, then any target Ω genuinely crossing that boundary satisfies

    Sol_AC(f;D,Ω)=∅.

The central equation is

    AC_{D→Ω}(F;f)
    =
    (
        ∂̄F,
        F|_D-f
    )
    =
    (0,0),

with the exact criterion

    Sol_AC(f;D,Ω)≠∅
    <=>
    f admits analytic continuation to Ω.

In the most general setting, where no single planar domain is fixed,

    Lift([f]_{z_0})
    =
    (R_f,π,\mathcal F),

where R_f contains all analytic germs genuinely reachable from the initial germ.

The entire idea can therefore be compressed into

    local function
        ->
    continuation equation
        ->
    all compatible continuations
        ->
    germ space
        ->
    Riemann surface
        ->
    maximal analytic continuation.

Thus infinitely many analytic-continuation possibilities do not necessarily destroy the
notion of maximal analytic continuation.

More precisely, they may force the "maximal object" to move from an ordinary planar domain
to a Riemann surface carrying all compatible continuation states.

This is the most rigorous mathematical connection between analytic continuation and the
idea of structural lifting of functions without making claims beyond standard complex
analysis.


